Contents

This is the fourth in the series of volumes detailing the statistical information collected during the 
Census of Population and Housing. The Census takes place every ten years. It gives a picture of the 
people, households and dwellings in Malta at a point in time, called Census Day in the terminology 
of the census. In the 18th Census held in 2021, Census Day was 21 November. Follow-ups of data 
collection, compilation, validation and analysis were carried out throughout 2022. The set of 
publications comprises:
Preliminary report August 2022
Population, migration and other social characteristics February 2023
Dwelling characteristics August 2023
Health, education, employment and other characteristics January 2024
Below are excerpts from this publication:
Marital status, Fertility, Gender diversity
• 50.4 per cent of the female resident population aged 16 and over resulted as either married or in a civil union.
• The proportion of single males exceeded that of single females, 42.8 per cent against 34.5 per cent of the respective 16+ 
cohorts.
• The mean age of mothers at their first live birth was 25.9 years, up from 24.9 years recorded in the 2011 Census.
• Mothers made up 64.2 per cent of the female resident population aged 15 and over.
• Of these, over 14,400 were single mothers. 
• The rate of individuals who identified themselves as gay, lesbian, bisexual or with a different sexual orientation was 5.5 
per cent among non-Maltese nationals against 1.6 per cent among Maltese nationals.
Typology of households
• The most prevalent household type emerging from the Census was the single-person household, characterised by 
individuals aged 30-64. This accounted for 18.1 per cent of households.
• 6,378 households were made up of single parents with one or more dependent children. This denoted an increase of 
over 900 such households recorded in the 2011 Census.
• The share of households with no dependent children went up to 76.1 per cent from around 65 per cent in 2011.
Health
• Over a quarter of the resident population aged five and over resulted as suffering from a long-term illness and/or chronic 
condition. It was noted that this marked a rise of six percentage points from 2011.
Education
• The shift to higher education continued to be upheld. All educational attainment levels above upper secondary were 
higher when compared to the 2011 Census.
• As regards tertiary level, the proportion of attainers aged 15 and over emerged as considerably higher than that recorded 
in 2011, with almost a quarter of individuals in this age bracket compared to 14.1 per cent in the previous Census.
• The literacy rate calculated from Census 2021 was 95.7 per cent. This implied that over 20,400 individuals aged 10 and 
over were illiterate.  In the Census, literacy was defined as the ability to read and write a short, simple sentence in any 
language. 
MR ETIENNE CARUANA
Census Officer
Introduction
Employment
• The decade 2011-2021 displayed a noteworthy increase in the labour force, from nearly 172,000 to 274,000 individuals 
aged 15 and over.
• Some trends of gender disparity as regards the nature of occupations persisted. 
• The predominant positions of male residents were professionals (16.4 per cent) technicians and associate professionals 
(14.9 per cent) and craft and related trade workers (13.7 per cent).
• Over a quarter of female residents worked as service and sales workers, and 14.2 per cent had clerical support positions.
Languages
• Maltese emerged as the most prevalent language spoken from early childhood across all age groups of Maltese nationals. 
• At the same time, the data indicated that nearly one-fourth of children under 10 years and around 15 per cent of 
individuals between 10-19 years considered English as their primary language from early childhood.
• The largest percentages of Maltese nationals aged five and over who spoke English from early childhood referred to 
residents of Is-Swieqi, Tas-Sliema, San Ġiljan, and L-Imdina.
Importance of the Census
The Census is important for the country’s residents because it provides information needed by the State and local authorities to 
plan and fund educational facilities, roads, healthcare and community services, among others. It also provides a benchmark for 
extrapolations of demographic and social data to be undertaken in the decade to come. As the office responsible for planning and 
running the Census, the NSO is confident that it has compiled an indicative vein of data on a range of disciplines that will meet the 
needs of all levels of users: policymakers, researchers, academics and the wider public. The Census volumes covers many aspects. 
That said, the NSO accepts requests for even more detailed information and will satisfy them to the extent possible. 
Acknowledgements
First and foremost, my sincere gratitude goes to the two Deputy Census Officers, Silvan Zammit and Matthew Zerafa, for 
their initiative and commitment throughout the Census journey. Equally, thanks are due to the staff of the Census Office and 
Methodology and Quality Unit who supervised and executed the fieldwork and data processes. I want to highlight the support 
received from the public entities and organisations which engaged with us in consultations and made available their administrative 
registers. Above all, a special mention must be made of the contribution of Malta’s resident population. Without the public’s 
cooperation and trust, the Census 2021 project would not have been possible.
   
       
 
Etienne CARUANA
Census Officer 
January 2024
