COMMENTARY
Household composition
The majority of households, accounting for 76.1 per cent, had no dependent children, marking an increase from 65 per cent in
2011. Indeed, the most prevalent household type was the single-person household, characterised by individuals aged 30-64,
with 18.1 per cent. In 2011, this figure stood at 11.5 per cent, with the highest proportion observed in other households without
dependent children (16.5 per cent). A total of 6,378 households were comprised of single parents with one or more dependent
children, an increase of 922 households compared to ten years before.
The dwelling preferences of one-person households were linked to the age of the occupant. Approximately one-third of those
aged 65 or older resided in terraced houses, while nearly 80 per cent of individuals aged under 30 residing alone, were living in
flats or penthouses. On the other hand, 33.9 per cent of lone households aged 65 or older resided in flats or penthouses, while
only 4.2 per

--- page 17 ----------------------------------------------------
17
Health condition
A total of 129,132 individuals, constituting 26 per cent of persons aged 5 years or more, suffered from a long-term illness,
disease, and/or chronic condition. This reflects a 6-percentage-point rise compared to the preceding Census, where the figure
was 82,208 persons, representing 20.7 per cent of the population. Health issues demonstrated a correlation with sex and age,
with females (27.4 per cent) experiencing a higher prevalence of such conditions than males (24.6 per cent).
Regarding age, 61.7 per cent of individuals aged 60 years and above were identified as suffering from a long-term illness, disease,
and/or chronic condition, signifying a notable increase over the past decade. Indeed, this stood at 46.7 per cent in 2011 when
the highest rate of persons suffering from the same conditions was slightly over 57 per cent, observed among persons aged 80
or more.
